**Factory Method** defines a creation step as a method that subclasses override to decide which concrete class gets built, while the surrounding algorithm stays in the base class unchanged.

```java
abstract class DialogBox {
    abstract Button createButton();      // factory method
    void render() {
        Button b = createButton();
        b.draw();
    }
}
class WindowsDialogBox extends DialogBox {
    Button createButton() { return new WindowsButton(); }
}
class MacDialogBox extends DialogBox {
    Button createButton() { return new MacButton(); }
}
```

`render()` never mentions `WindowsButton` or `MacButton` — it only knows `createButton()` returns *a* `Button`. Adding a new platform means adding one subclass, not editing `render()`.

Takeaway: Factory Method pushes the "which class" decision down to a subclass, one product at a time.

**Abstract Factory** goes one level up: instead of one factory method returning one product, it's a factory *object* with several creation methods, each returning one member of a matched family.

```java
interface UiFactory {
    Button createButton();
    Checkbox createCheckbox();
}
class DarkUiFactory implements UiFactory {
    Button createButton() { return new DarkButton(); }
    Checkbox createCheckbox() { return new DarkCheckbox(); }
}
```

The guarantee Abstract Factory adds over Factory Method: every product it hands out comes from the *same* family, so you can never accidentally pair a `DarkButton` with a `LightCheckbox`.

Takeaway: use Abstract Factory when products must be created in matched sets, not one at a time.
